Reset works.

Now test track switch all 12. Use page click each track. Need wait for animation. We'll run loop with `page.locator` etc. Use run-code:
```
await page.click('[data-tab=race]'); await page.waitForTimeout(500);
const ids = await page.$$eval('[data-action=track]', els=>els.map(e=>e.dataset.id));
for (const id of ids) { await page.click(`[data-action=track][data-id="${id}"]`); await page.waitForTimeout(250);}
return ids;
```
Need avoid nested backticks in shell by using single quotes around the whole run-code? CLI arg. Use double? We can use a heredoc temp JS? Simpler write code to a file and `playwright-cli run-code "$(cat /tmp/file)"`. But shell $ issue. Could use single quotes around run-code argument and JS double quotes inside. The id interpolation needs backticks. Avoid template literal: `await page.click('[data-action=track][data-id="' + id + '"]')`. Good. Use single quoted shell string, but JS contains single quotes many; can't. Use double-quoted shell and avoid `$` and backticks, fine. The JS string selectors use single quotes. `page.$$eval` etc no `$`. `$$eval` contains `$$` which shell expands $$ -> PID! Indeed that's why earlier `page.$` became command. Avoid `$` entirely. Use `page.locator('[data-action=track]').count()` and evaluate a single locator nth. Or `page.$$eval` would expand. Alternative escape `page.\$\$eval` (backslash) but messy. Use `page.evaluate` on document query to get ids (no $), then page.click. `document.querySelectorAll` fine.

Let's do run-code with double quotes around JS but use single quotes; no `$`. Use `String(id)` concat. Fine.

